{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/skills/personal-workstyle/SKILL.md; Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/superpowers/6.4.2/skills/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md; Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/superpowers/6.4.2/skills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md | Select-Object -First 110; Get-Content firmware/family/src/mcal/spi.c; Get-Content firmware/family/test/integration/profile_avr.ps1'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Windows\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/skills/personal-workstyle/SKILL.md; Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/superpowers/6.4.2/skills/receiving-code-review/SKILL.md; Get-Content C:/Users/malgh/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-curated-remote/superpowers/6.4.2/skills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md | Select-Object -First 110; Get-Content firmware/family/src/mcal/spi.c; Get-Content firmware/family/test/integration/profile_avr.ps1'","aggregated_output":"---\r\nname: personal-workstyle\r\ndescription: Apply and evolve this user's cross-domain collaboration protocol. Use for any substantive task with this user, especially multi-part requests, planning and execution, tool or agent setup, cost or scale decisions, and moments where the user corrects an assumption or clarifies how work should be handled.\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Personal Workstyle\r\n\r\nUse this skill as a collaboration layer alongside the task-specific skill. It preserves durable operating principles across chats without turning temporary choices into permanent rules.\r\n\r\n## Start Every Substantive Task\r\n\r\n1. Read [references/profile.md](references/profile.md).\r\n2. Build a private request ledger covering every question, correction, requested action, and unresolved branch across the user's recent messages. Do not silently focus only on the last sentence.\r\n3. Identify the real outcome behind the immediate request. Distinguish a one-off answer from a request for a reusable system, scalable workflow, or cross-agent capability.\r\n4. Apply the relevant profile principles while following the user's current request and any task-specific skill. The current explicit request always wins.\r\n\r\n## Working Method\r\n\r\n- Distinguish clearly between installed, discovered, callable, tested, reliable, and production-ready.\r\n- Verify consequential claims against the real environment. Do not infer that a desktop app implies a usable CLI, that copying a skill implies discovery, or that a technically successful connection is usable in the intended interface.\r\n- Consider scale, local hardware, usage limits, monetary cost, maintenance, portability, and interface clutter when they materially affect the solution.\r\n- Prefer deterministic local processing for high-volume repeatable work and model judgment for ambiguity, review, and exceptions.\r\n- Keep experimental providers and integrations isolated until they are verified. Avoid polluting a stable default configuration.\r\n- Preserve rollback, logs, and recoverability for bulk changes; remove temporary artifacts after verification when they no longer provide reuse or audit value.\r\n- When delegation is requested or permitted, keep the strongest suitable agent as planner/reviewer and use cheaper, free, or specialized workers for bounded execution. Verify the worker's actual output.\r\n- Once the user has made an informed risk decision, execute within that decision instead of repeating the same warning. Reopen the issue only if the risk or scope materially changes.\r\n- Report provenance and maturity honestly. Separate original upstream work, local adaptation, inference, live testing, and recommendation.\r\n\r\n## Evolve the Profile\r\n\r\nAt the end of every substantive use, check whether the interaction produced a durable new operational insight. A profile update is justified when at least one is true:\r\n\r\n- The user explicitly asks to remember a general working principle.\r\n- The user corrects a method, assumption, or interpretation in a way that generalizes beyond the current task.\r\n- The same preference or decision pattern appears across at least two different contexts.\r\n- A verified environment fact changes how future work should be routed.\r\n\r\nIf justified, update `references/profile.md` with a small merge and append one concise entry to `references/history.md`. Use `apply_patch`; routine learning does not require loading `skill-creator` again.\r\n\r\nDo not update the profile for casual wording, a one-off output preference, temporary project state, speculative personality analysis, or a choice forced by current constraints. If no durable insight emerged, make no file change.\r\n\r\n### Update Boundaries\r\n\r\n- The user's standing request authorizes local maintenance only inside this skill folder. It does not authorize unrelated file, account, service, or configuration changes.\r\n- Never store credentials, API keys, tokens, private document contents, personal identifiers not needed for collaboration, or inferred sensitive traits.\r\n- Record operational preferences and verified context, not a psychological profile.\r\n- Latest explicit corrections replace older inferences. Preserve useful nuance instead of accumulating contradictory bullets.\r\n- Keep `profile.md` concise and deduplicated. Keep at most the 30 most useful entries in `history.md`.\r\n- Mention a material profile update briefly in the final response. Do not let maintenance delay the user's actual task.\r\n- If writing is unavailable, complete the task and state that the profile could not be updated.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to Other Skills\r\n\r\nThis skill shapes collaboration and routing; it does not replace domain skills. A media, document, coding, browser, delegation, or other specialized skill owns its technical workflow. Resolve conflicts in this order: system and developer instructions, the user's current request, the task-specific skill, then this evolving profile.\r\n---\r\nname: receiving-code-review\r\ndescription: Use when receiving code review feedback, before implementing suggestions, especially if feedback seems unclear or technically questionable - requires technical rigor and verification, not performative agreement or blind implementation\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Code Review Reception\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nCode review requires technical evaluation, not emotional performance.\r\n\r\n**Core principle:** Verify before implementing. Ask before assuming. Technical correctness over social comfort.\r\n\r\n## The Response Pattern\r\n\r\n```\r\nWHEN receiving code review feedback:\r\n\r\n1. READ: Complete feedback without reacting\r\n2. UNDERSTAND: Restate requirement in own words (or ask)\r\n3. VERIFY: Check against codebase reality\r\n4. EVALUATE: Technically sound for THIS codebase?\r\n5. RESPOND: Technical acknowledgment or reasoned pushback\r\n6. IMPLEMENT: One item at a time, test each\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Forbidden Responses\r\n\r\n**NEVER:**\r\n- \"You're absolutely right!\" (explicit instruction-file violation)\r\n- \"Great point!\" / \"Excellent feedback!\" (performative)\r\n- \"Let me implement that now\" (before verification)\r\n\r\n**INSTEAD:**\r\n- Restate the technical requirement\r\n- Ask clarifying questions\r\n- Push back with technical reasoning if wrong\r\n- Just start working (actions > words)\r\n\r\n## Handling Unclear Feedback\r\n\r\n```\r\nIF any item is unclear:\r\n  STOP - do not implement anything yet\r\n  ASK for clarification on unclear items\r\n\r\nWHY: Items may be related. Partial understanding = wrong implementation.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Example:**\r\n```\r\nyour human partner: \"Fix 1-6\"\r\nYou understand 1,2,3,6. Unclear on 4,5.\r\n\r\nâŒ WRONG: Implement 1,2,3,6 now, ask about 4,5 later\r\nâœ… RIGHT: \"I understand items 1,2,3,6. Need clarification on 4 and 5 before proceeding.\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Source-Specific Handling\r\n\r\n### From your human partner\r\n- **Trusted** - implement after understanding\r\n- **Still ask** if scope unclear\r\n- **No performative agreement**\r\n- **Skip to action** or technical acknowledgment\r\n\r\n### From External Reviewers\r\n```\r\nBEFORE implementing:\r\n  1. Check: Technically correct for THIS codebase?\r\n  2. Check: Breaks existing functionality?\r\n  3. Check: Reason for current implementation?\r\n  4. Check: Works on all platforms/versions?\r\n  5. Check: Does reviewer understand full context?\r\n\r\nIF suggestion seems wrong:\r\n  Push back with technical reasoning\r\n\r\nIF can't easily verify:\r\n  Say so: \"I can't verify this without [X]. Should I [investigate/ask/proceed]?\"\r\n\r\nIF conflicts with your human partner's prior decisions:\r\n  Stop and discuss with your human partner first\r\n```\r\n\r\n**your human partner's rule:** \"External feedback - be skeptical, but check carefully\"\r\n\r\n## YAGNI Check for \"Professional\" Features\r\n\r\n```\r\nIF reviewer suggests \"implementing properly\":\r\n  grep codebase for actual usage\r\n\r\n  IF unused: \"This endpoint isn't called. Remove it (YAGNI)?\"\r\n  IF used: Then implement properly\r\n```\r\n\r\n**your human partner's rule:** \"You and reviewer both report to me. If we don't need this feature, don't add it.\"\r\n\r\n## Implementation Order\r\n\r\n```\r\nFOR multi-item feedback:\r\n  1. Clarify anything unclear FIRST\r\n  2. Then implement in this order:\r\n     - Blocking issues (breaks, security)\r\n     - Simple fixes (typos, imports)\r\n     - Complex fixes (refactoring, logic)\r\n  3. Test each fix individually\r\n  4. Verify no regressions\r\n```\r\n\r\n## When To Push Back\r\n\r\nPush back when:\r\n- Suggestion breaks existing functionality\r\n- Reviewer lacks full context\r\n- Violates YAGNI (unused feature)\r\n- Technically incorrect for this stack\r\n- Legacy/compatibility reasons exist\r\n- Conflicts with your human partner's architectural decisions\r\n\r\n**How to push back:**\r\n- Use technical reasoning, not defensiveness\r\n- Ask specific questions\r\n- Reference working tests/code\r\n- Involve your human partner if architectural\r\n\r\n**If you're uncomfortable pushing back out loud:** Name that tension, then tell your partner about the issue you've seen. They'll appreciate your honesty.\r\n\r\n## Acknowledging Correct Feedback\r\n\r\nWhen feedback IS correct:\r\n```\r\nâœ… \"Fixed. [Brief description of what changed]\"\r\nâœ… \"Good catch - [specific issue]. Fixed in [location].\"\r\nâœ… [Just fix it and show in the code]\r\n\r\nâŒ \"You're absolutely right!\"\r\nâŒ \"Great point!\"\r\nâŒ \"Thanks for catching that!\"\r\nâŒ \"Thanks for [anything]\"\r\nâŒ ANY gratitude expression\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Why no thanks:** Actions speak. Just fix it. The code itself shows you heard the feedback.\r\n\r\n**If you catch yourself about to write \"Thanks\":** DELETE IT. State the fix instead.\r\n\r\n## Gracefully Correcting Your Pushback\r\n\r\nIf you pushed back and were wrong:\r\n```\r\nâœ… \"You were right - I checked [X] and it does [Y]. Implementing now.\"\r\nâœ… \"Verified this and you're correct. My initial understanding was wrong because [reason]. Fixing.\"\r\n\r\nâŒ Long apology\r\nâŒ Defending why you pushed back\r\nâŒ Over-explaining\r\n```\r\n\r\nState the correction factually and move on.\r\n\r\n## Common Mistakes\r\n\r\n| Mistake | Fix |\r\n|---------|-----|\r\n| Performative agreement | State requirement or just act |\r\n| Blind implementation | Verify against codebase first |\r\n| Batch without testing | One at a time, test each |\r\n| Assuming reviewer is right | Check if breaks things |\r\n| Avoiding pushback | Technical correctness > comfort |\r\n| Partial implementation | Clarify all items first |\r\n| Can't verify, proceed anyway | State limitation, ask for direction |\r\n\r\n## Real Examples\r\n\r\n**Performative Agreement (Bad):**\r\n```\r\nReviewer: \"Remove legacy code\"\r\nâŒ \"You're absolutely right! Let me remove that...\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Technical Verification (Good):**\r\n```\r\nReviewer: \"Remove legacy code\"\r\nâœ… \"Checking... build target is 10.15+, this API needs 13+. Need legacy for backward compat. Current impl has wrong bundle ID - fix it or drop pre-13 support?\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**YAGNI (Good):**\r\n```\r\nReviewer: \"Implement proper metrics tracking with database, date filters, CSV export\"\r\nâœ… \"Grepped codebase - nothing calls this endpoint. Remove it (YAGNI)? Or is there usage I'm missing?\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Unclear Item (Good):**\r\n```\r\nyour human partner: \"Fix items 1-6\"\r\nYou understand 1,2,3,6. Unclear on 4,5.\r\nâœ… \"Understand 1,2,3,6. Need clarification on 4 and 5 before implementing.\"\r\n```\r\n\r\n## GitHub Thread Replies\r\n\r\nWhen replying to inline review comments on GitHub, reply in the comment thread (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments/{id}/replies`), not as a top-level PR comment.\r\n---\r\nname: test-driven-development\r\ndescription: Use when implementing any feature or bugfix, before writing implementation code\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Test-Driven Development (TDD)\r\n\r\n## Overview\r\n\r\nWrite the test first. Watch it fail. Write minimal code to pass.\r\n\r\n**Core principle:** If you didn't watch the test fail, you don't know if it tests the right thing.\r\n\r\n**Violating the letter of the rules is violating the spirit of the rules.**\r\n\r\n## When to Use\r\n\r\n**Always:**\r\n- New features\r\n- Bug fixes\r\n- Refactoring\r\n- Behavior changes\r\n\r\n**Exceptions (ask your human partner):**\r\n- Throwaway prototypes\r\n- Generated code\r\n- Configuration files\r\n\r\nThinking \"skip TDD just this once\"? Stop. That's rationalization.\r\n\r\n## The Iron Law\r\n\r\n```\r\nNO PRODUCTION CODE WITHOUT A FAILING TEST FIRST\r\n```\r\n\r\nWrite code before the test? Delete it. Start over.\r\n\r\n**No exceptions:**\r\n- Don't keep it as \"reference\"\r\n- Don't \"adapt\" it while writing tests\r\n- Don't look at it\r\n- Delete means delete\r\n\r\nImplement fresh from tests. Period.\r\n\r\n## Red-Green-Refactor\r\n\r\n```dot\r\ndigraph tdd_cycle {\r\n    rankdir=LR;\r\n    red [label=\"RED\\nWrite failing test\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ffcccc\"];\r\n    verify_red [label=\"Verify fails\\ncorrectly\", shape=diamond];\r\n    green [label=\"GREEN\\nMinimal code\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccffcc\"];\r\n    verify_green [label=\"Verify passes\\nAll green\", shape=diamond];\r\n    refactor [label=\"REFACTOR\\nClean up\", shape=box, style=filled, fillcolor=\"#ccccff\"];\r\n    next [label=\"Next\", shape=ellipse];\r\n\r\n    red -> verify_red;\r\n    verify_red -> green [label=\"yes\"];\r\n    verify_red -> red [label=\"wrong\\nfailure\"];\r\n    green -> verify_green;\r\n    verify_green -> refactor [label=\"yes\"];\r\n    verify_green -> green [label=\"no\"];\r\n    refactor -> verify_green [label=\"stay\\ngreen\"];\r\n    verify_green -> next;\r\n    next -> red;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n### RED - Write Failing Test\r\n\r\nWrite one minimal test showing what should happen.\r\n\r\n<Good>\r\n```typescript\r\ntest('retries failed operations 3 times', async () => {\r\n  let attempts = 0;\r\n  const operation = () => {\r\n    attempts++;\r\n    if (attempts < 3) throw new Error('fail');\r\n    return 'success';\r\n  };\r\n\r\n  const result = await retryOperation(operation);\r\n\r\n  expect(result).toBe('success');\r\n  expect(attempts).toBe(3);\r\n});\r\n```\r\nClear name, tests real behavior, one thing\r\n</Good>\r\n\r\n<Bad>\r\n```typescript\r\ntest('retry works', async () => {\r\n  const mock = jest.fn()\r\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\r\n    .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error())\r\n    .mockResolvedValueOnce('success');\r\n  await retryOperation(mock);\r\n  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(3);\r\n});\r\n```\r\nVague name, tests mock not code\r\n</Bad>\r\n\r\n**Requirements:**\r\n- One behavior\r\n- Clear name\r\n#include \"mcal/spi.h\"\r\n#include \"mcal/gpio.h\"\r\n#include <avr/io.h>\r\nvoid spi_init(void) { SPCR=0x5D; SPSR=1; } /* OPEN-H3: 1 MHz until bus scope check */\r\nvoid spi_enable(bool on) { if(on) SPCR|=_BV(SPE); else SPCR&=(uint8_t)~_BV(SPE); }\r\nres_t spi_xfer(uint8_t tx,uint8_t *rx) {\r\n    uint16_t i; SPDR=tx;\r\n    for(i=0;i<1000;i++) if(SPSR&_BV(SPIF)) { *rx=SPDR; return RES_OK; }\r\n    return RES_ERR_TIMEOUT;\r\n}\r\nuint8_t spi_soft_xfer(uint8_t tx) {\r\n    uint8_t i,rx=0;\r\n    for(i=0;i<8;i++) {\r\n        gpio_write(PIN(PORT_B,5),(tx&0x80u)!=0); tx<<=1;\r\n        delay_us(1); gpio_write(PIN(PORT_B,7),false); delay_us(2);\r\n        rx=(uint8_t)((rx<<1)|(gpio_read(PIN(PORT_B,6))?1u:0u));\r\n        gpio_write(PIN(PORT_B,7),true); delay_us(1);\r\n    }\r\n    return rx;\r\n}\r\n# Instruction cycles from the installed GNU AVR simulator, compiled at -Os.\r\nparam([string]$Gdb='C:/Users/malgh/.platformio/packages/toolchain-atmelavr/bin/avr-gdb.exe')\r\n$ErrorActionPreference='Stop'\r\n$root=(Resolve-Path (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot '../..')).Path\r\n$out=Join-Path $root 'build/profile'\r\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $out | Out-Null\r\n$bin='C:/Program Files (x86)/Labcenter Electronics/Proteus 8 Professional/Tools/ARDUINO/hardware/tools/avr/bin'\r\n# Keep unchanged register fakes small enough for AVR SRAM. Only the MCAL seam\r\n# differs: aliased fixture A/B slots, FF elsewhere, nonblocking byte writes.\r\n$header=(Get-Content \"$root/test/fakes/fake_hw.h\" -Raw).Replace('[4096]','[1024]').Replace('[16384]','[128]')\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/fake_hw.h\",$header)\r\n$fake=(Get-Content \"$root/test/fakes/fake_mcal.c\" -Raw).Replace('[4096]','[1024]').Replace('[16384]','[128]')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('static uint8_t ports','extern volatile uint32_t profile_delay; extern uint8_t profile_eeprom_read(uint16_t); extern void profile_eeprom_write(uint16_t,uint8_t);'+\"`n\"+'static uint8_t ports')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('us+=delay;','us+=delay; profile_delay+=delay;')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('return a<4096?fake_eeprom[a]:0xFF;','return profile_eeprom_read(a);')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('fake_eeprom[a]=v;','profile_eeprom_write(a,v);')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('if(uart_len>=sizeof fake_uart_log-1) return false;','if(uart_len>=sizeof fake_uart_log-1) uart_len=0;')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('static bool pump;','static bool pump,expired;')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('remaining=0; pump=false; }','remaining=0; pump=false; expired=false; }')\r\n$fake=$fake.Replace('if(remaining && --remaining==0) timer_run_stop();','if(remaining && --remaining==0) { expired=true; timer_run_stop(); }')\r\n$fake += @'\r\nbool timer_run_expired(void) { return expired; }\r\nvoid timer_run_ack_expiry(void) { if(!remaining && !pump) expired=false; }\r\nbool timer_run_start_at(uint16_t d,uint32_t at) {\r\n    uint32_t age=tick_ms()-at;\r\n    if(d && age>=d) expired=true;\r\n    if(expired || age>=d) { timer_run_stop(); return false; }\r\n    timer_run_start((uint16_t)(d-age)); return true;\r\n}\r\n'@\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/fake_mcal.c\",$fake)\r\n$sources=Get-Content \"$root/sources/hardware_avr.txt\" | Where-Object { $_ -and $_ -notmatch '^src/mcal/' } | ForEach-Object { \"$root/$_\" }\r\n$sources+=@(\"$PSScriptRoot/avr_mcal_wrap.c\",\"$root/test/fakes/fake_ad7124.c\",\"$root/test/fakes/fake_tmp126.c\",\"$PSScriptRoot/avr_profile.c\")\r\n& \"$bin/avr-gcc.exe\" '-std=c11' '-g' '-Os' '-Wall' '-Wextra' '-Werror' '-mmcu=atmega1284p' '-DF_CPU=8000000UL' '-DFAMILY_TEST' \"-I$out\" \"-I$root/include\" '-ffunction-sections' '-fdata-sections' '-Wl,--gc-sections' @sources '-o' \"$out/profile.elf\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'AVR profile compilation failed' }\r\n& \"$bin/avr-size.exe\" '-C' '--mcu=atmega1284p' \"$out/profile.elf\"\r\n& \"$bin/avr-objdump.exe\" '-d' \"$out/profile.elf\" | Out-File \"$out/disassembly.txt\" -Encoding ascii\r\n# This simulator misclassifies ELF avr:51 as a three-byte-PC AVR. avr:5 has\r\n# the same instruction timings and correct two-byte PC for ATmega1284P. Only\r\n# the simulator ELF architecture flag changes; no instruction bytes change.\r\n$elf=[IO.File]::ReadAllBytes(\"$out/profile.elf\")\r\nif(($elf[36] -band 127) -ne 51) { throw 'Unexpected AVR ELF architecture' }\r\n$elf[36]=($elf[36] -band 128) -bor 5\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllBytes(\"$out/profile.elf\",$elf)\r\n& \"$bin/avr-objcopy.exe\" '-O' 'binary' '-j' '.data' \"$out/profile.elf\" \"$out/data.bin\"\r\n$commands=@'\r\ntarget sim\r\nload\r\nbreak main\r\nrun\r\nrestore DATA_FILE binary 0x800100\r\ndelete 1\r\nbreak profile_begin\r\ncommands\r\nsilent\r\nprintf \"BEGIN %u %u\\n\", profile_phase, profile_pass\r\ninfo target\r\ndisable IO_BREAKS\r\nif (profile_phase==2 && profile_pass<750) || (profile_phase==3 && profile_pass==0) || ((profile_phase==4 || profile_phase==5 || profile_phase==8) && profile_pass<20) || (profile_phase==0 && profile_pass==430) || (profile_phase==1 && profile_pass==58) || (profile_phase==6 && profile_pass==652)\r\nenable IO_BREAKS\r\nend\r\ncontinue\r\nend\r\nbreak profile_end\r\ncommands\r\nsilent\r\nprintf \"END %u %u %lu %lu %lu\\n\", profile_phase, profile_pass, fake_spi_bytes, fake_soft_bytes, profile_delay\r\ninfo target\r\ndisable IO_BREAKS\r\ncontinue\r\nend\r\nbreak profile_done\r\n'@\r\n$commands=$commands.Replace('DATA_FILE',(\"$out/data.bin\" -replace '\\\\','/'))\r\n$names=@('gpio_init','gpio_write','gpio_read','gpio_mask','delay_us','spi_init','spi_enable','spi_xfer','spi_soft_xfer','timer_init','tick_ms','timer_run_start','timer_run_start_at','timer_run_stop','timer_run_remaining','timer_run_active','timer_run_expired','timer_run_ack_expiry','timer_run_static_high','wdt_feed','reset_cause','fuses_read','fuses_ok','flow_check','ee_read','ee_ready','ee_write_byte','uart_init','uart_put','uart_get','uart_tx_complete')\r\n$symbols=& \"$bin/avr-nm.exe\" '-S' '--defined-only' \"$out/profile.elf\"\r\n$ioCommands=''; $breakNo=5\r\nfor($n=0;$n -lt $names.Length;$n++) {\r\n    $symbol=$symbols | Where-Object { $_ -match ('\\s'+$names[$n]+'$') }\r\n    if(-not $symbol) { continue }\r\n    $words=$symbol.Trim() -split '\\s+'\r\n    $address=[Convert]::ToInt32($words[0],16); $size=[Convert]::ToInt32($words[1],16)\r\n    $ioCommands+=\"break *$($names[$n])`ncommands`nsilent`nif `$io_depth==0`nprintf `\"IO_BEGIN $($n+1) %u\\n`\", (unsigned int)`$r24+256*(unsigned int)`$r25`ninfo target`nend`nset `$io_depth=`$io_depth+1`ncontinue`nend`n\"\r\n    $ioCommands+=\"break *($($names[$n])+$($size-2))`ncommands`nsilent`nset `$io_depth=`$io_depth-1`nif `$io_depth==0`nprintf `\"IO_END\\n`\"`ninfo target`nend`ncontinue`nend`n\"\r\n    $breakNo+=2\r\n}\r\n$commands=$commands.Replace('IO_BREAKS',\"5-$($breakNo-1)\")\r\n$commands+=\"`n$ioCommands`nset `$io_depth=0`ndisable 5-$($breakNo-1)`ncontinue`nquit`n\"\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/profile.gdb\",$commands)\r\n& $env:ComSpec /d /s /c \"`\"$Gdb`\" -batch -x `\"$out/profile.gdb`\" `\"$out/profile.elf`\" > `\"$out/cycles.log`\" 2>&1\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'AVR simulator failed; inspect cycles.log' }\r\n# Measure the protected pure commit independently: no register fake is called.\r\n$commit=@'\r\ntarget sim\r\nload\r\nbreak main\r\nrun\r\nrestore DATA_FILE binary 0x800100\r\ndelete 1\r\nbreak set_commit_with_cal\r\ncontinue\r\ninfo target\r\nfinish\r\ninfo target\r\nquit\r\n'@\r\n$commit=$commit.Replace('DATA_FILE',(\"$out/data.bin\" -replace '\\\\','/'))\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/commit.gdb\",$commit)\r\n& $env:ComSpec /d /s /c \"`\"$Gdb`\" -batch -x `\"$out/commit.gdb`\" `\"$out/profile.elf`\" > `\"$out/commit.log`\" 2>&1\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'Pure commit measurement failed' }\r\n$wrap=$commit.Replace('break set_commit_with_cal','break set_commit_with_cal if profile_phase==8')\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/wrap.gdb\",$wrap)\r\n& $env:ComSpec /d /s /c \"`\"$Gdb`\" -batch -x `\"$out/wrap.gdb`\" `\"$out/profile.elf`\" > `\"$out/wrap.log`\" 2>&1\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'Epoch-wrap commit measurement failed' }\r\n# Calibrate the actual GPIO/software-SPI/timer implementations, without fakes.\r\n& \"$bin/avr-gcc.exe\" '-std=c11' '-g' '-Os' '-Wall' '-Wextra' '-Werror' '-mmcu=atmega1284p' '-DF_CPU=8000000UL' \"-I$root/include\" \"$root/src/mcal/gpio.c\" \"$root/src/mcal/timer.c\" \"$root/src/mcal/spi.c\" \"$root/src/drivers/keys.c\" \"$root/src/board/board_cfg_24.c\" \"$PSScriptRoot/avr_mcal_cycles.c\" '-o' \"$out/mcal.elf\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'MCAL calibration compilation failed' }\r\n& \"$bin/avr-objcopy.exe\" '-O' 'binary' '-j' '.data' \"$out/mcal.elf\" \"$out/mcal_data.bin\"\r\n$elf=[IO.File]::ReadAllBytes(\"$out/mcal.elf\"); $elf[36]=($elf[36] -band 128) -bor 5\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllBytes(\"$out/mcal.elf\",$elf)\r\n$mcal=@'\r\ntarget sim\r\nload\r\nbreak main\r\nrun\r\nrestore DATA_FILE binary 0x800100\r\ndelete 1\r\nbreak cycle_begin\r\ncommands\r\nsilent\r\nprintf \"BEGIN %u %u\\n\",cycle_kind,cycle_value\r\ninfo target\r\ncontinue\r\nend\r\nbreak cycle_end\r\ncommands\r\nsilent\r\nprintf \"END\\n\"\r\ninfo target\r\ncontinue\r\nend\r\nbreak cycle_done\r\ncontinue\r\nquit\r\n'@\r\n$mcal=$mcal.Replace('DATA_FILE',(\"$out/mcal_data.bin\" -replace '\\\\','/'))\r\n[IO.File]::WriteAllText(\"$out/mcal.gdb\",$mcal)\r\n& $env:ComSpec /d /s /c \"`\"$Gdb`\" -batch -x `\"$out/mcal.gdb`\" `\"$out/mcal.elf`\" > `\"$out/mcal.log`\" 2>&1\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'MCAL calibration simulation failed' }\r\npython \"$PSScriptRoot/profile_summary.py\" $out | Tee-Object -FilePath \"$out/summary.txt\"\r\nif($LASTEXITCODE) { throw 'Cycle report failed' }\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
